WEBVTT

00:03.878 --> 00:07.173
We did it! We made diamonds!

00:08.842 --> 00:12.220
We actually made them from scratch
with our own hands!

00:12.303 --> 00:13.888
We'd be millionaires!

00:15.473 --> 00:18.184
We've finally made it...

00:18.268 --> 00:21.229
They're the size of sand grains, though.

00:21.312 --> 00:23.148
They're ridiculously small,

00:23.231 --> 00:26.151
but I'm ten billion percent sure
they're diamonds.

00:28.069 --> 00:30.280
Looks like we can finally see it.

00:30.780 --> 00:35.368
The grand revival
of our fully-charged Medusa...

00:36.578 --> 00:38.121
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

02:14.843 --> 02:17.053
So, since they've made the diamonds,

02:17.679 --> 02:20.723
all that's left is to slot them in
and they'll be revived, right?

02:20.807 --> 02:22.475
Not yet.

02:23.226 --> 02:27.730
The ones we've got now aren't
big enough for Medusa's batteries.

02:27.814 --> 02:29.524
How can we make bigger ones from these?

02:29.607 --> 02:33.778
Well, human power alone
won't make us big diamonds at all.

02:33.862 --> 02:34.904
So...

02:35.613 --> 02:37.282
So?

02:37.907 --> 02:39.993
We'll go mine natural diamonds!

02:42.245 --> 02:45.665
Why did we bother
to make diamonds ourselves, then?

02:45.748 --> 02:48.418
- What was all that hardship for?
- I'll fricking kill you!

02:48.501 --> 02:51.754
We need both types, idiots.

02:52.338 --> 02:55.133
<i>Diamonds are carbon, like coal.</i>

02:55.216 --> 02:58.094
<i>They're just rocks lying around on Earth.</i>

02:58.177 --> 03:03.016
<i>It was only when humans came along
that a simple rock became a treasure.</i>

03:06.519 --> 03:08.271
The diamonds are here!

03:08.354 --> 03:10.940
If we send an expedition
from Xeno's fortress,

03:11.024 --> 03:12.859
they'll get there in a week.

03:13.443 --> 03:15.028
It's called Mount Lassen.

03:15.111 --> 03:18.615
A lava dome, a shield volcano,
a cinder cone, and a stratovolcano

03:18.698 --> 03:21.910
all packed together, it makes me LOL!
Of course we're gonna explore it!

03:21.993 --> 03:24.537
Its last eruption was around 1900,

03:24.621 --> 03:26.956
and it spewed out kimberlite magma!

03:27.040 --> 03:29.292
I've seen them, the kimberlite ores!

03:29.375 --> 03:31.586
There weren't many,
but it's been thousands of years!

03:31.669 --> 03:32.712
We totally got this!

03:33.713 --> 03:34.672
What?

03:34.756 --> 03:40.053
Yes, she means she knows a place where
we could mine a few natural diamonds.

03:44.474 --> 03:48.269
So we're still mining diamonds
even though we crafted them ourselves.

03:51.981 --> 03:53.650
A real diamond!

03:54.400 --> 03:56.277
Here's an entertaining quiz.

03:56.361 --> 03:58.947
Between natural and human-made diamonds,

03:59.030 --> 04:00.907
which do you think is tougher?

04:00.990 --> 04:04.077
I know this! The natural ones!

04:04.160 --> 04:06.996
I'm betting on the ones
I precisely crafted using science.

04:07.622 --> 04:09.540
Oh, here it is.

04:09.624 --> 04:12.210
The common misconception
that natural things

04:12.293 --> 04:14.128
are always better than human-made ones.

04:14.212 --> 04:16.089
How ironic.

04:16.172 --> 04:19.717
It's the person untouched
by the flood of 21st-century info

04:19.801 --> 04:22.011
who really grasps the core.

04:22.095 --> 04:25.098
Yes, the answer is human-made diamonds.

04:25.682 --> 04:27.725
One hundred trillion points to Chrome.

04:28.893 --> 04:32.188
Diamonds are the hardest thing on Earth.

04:32.272 --> 04:34.315
They can only be cut by other diamonds.

04:35.024 --> 04:39.279
<i>Since human-made diamonds are
ridiculously tougher than natural ones,</i>

04:39.362 --> 04:43.408
<i>we'll bundle a huge amount of them
together and make a file out of it.</i>

04:44.617 --> 04:46.035
I see!

04:46.119 --> 04:50.123
We'll use the tiny artificial diamonds
to polish the big natural ones,

04:50.206 --> 04:54.752
making new diamond batteries
that fit perfectly into the Medusas.

04:55.837 --> 05:00.174
We just need to make exact copies
of this blackened one.

05:00.258 --> 05:01.926
Leave it to a pro like me.

05:02.010 --> 05:05.346
No one in the universe can carve it
more precisely than me.

05:05.430 --> 05:06.931
We'll leave it to you.

05:07.015 --> 05:09.642
All we can do is give our support.

05:09.726 --> 05:12.228
He doesn't seem to get used to it.

05:13.062 --> 05:14.105
<i>Then...</i>

05:14.772 --> 05:17.442
This is the last transmission you'll hear

05:17.525 --> 05:19.027
from the World Expedition Team.

05:20.069 --> 05:21.696
Wait, what?

05:22.280 --> 05:23.740
If Stanley and the others

05:23.823 --> 05:26.326
circled South America and caught us...

05:26.409 --> 05:29.162
He's a vindictive and angry man.

05:29.245 --> 05:31.289
That's probably true
nine times out of ten.

05:31.914 --> 05:35.752
<i>As its captain, I can read Perseus's
movements like the back of my hand.</i>

05:36.544 --> 05:38.546
<i>They should be closing in right about now.</i>

05:39.130 --> 05:41.257
<i>A nearby radar wave
would expose our position,</i>

05:41.841 --> 05:44.927
<i>rendering it pointless
to build a stealth ship.</i>

05:45.595 --> 05:48.014
Next time we get in touch will be

05:48.848 --> 05:51.768
when everything's settled.

05:53.811 --> 05:55.104
By the way, Joel.

05:55.188 --> 05:58.232
Since we can listen
to one-way communications,

05:58.316 --> 06:01.110
so once you've perfected the technique
to build diamond batteries,

06:01.194 --> 06:03.196
just send us a message.

06:03.821 --> 06:07.867
<i>That's a borderline way
of not revealing our true goal.</i>

06:07.950 --> 06:09.827
<i>We want to figure out</i>

06:09.911 --> 06:14.332
<i>how to use this huge amount of Medusas
to fight Stanley, after all.</i>

06:24.217 --> 06:27.970
Even if Joel learns how to make
diamond batteries for the Medusas

06:28.054 --> 06:29.972
and then teaches us,

06:30.056 --> 06:32.558
how are we supposed to actually make them?

06:32.642 --> 06:35.353
We have no examples to follow.

06:35.436 --> 06:38.189
By the way,
where's this stealth ship headed now?

06:38.272 --> 06:40.566
I know! It's Araxá!

06:40.650 --> 06:42.276
Araxá?

06:43.402 --> 06:46.447
Correct. I give you 1,000 trillion points.

06:47.323 --> 06:49.575
<i>Araxá, in the heart of Brazil.</i>

06:49.659 --> 06:51.202
<i>A miraculous mineral vein</i>

06:51.869 --> 06:55.581
<i>nurtured by the vast Amazon
and its ridiculously long river.</i>

06:56.165 --> 07:00.878
<i>It's a rocky hallowed ground where
rare metals flowed in and accumulated.</i>

07:00.962 --> 07:02.713
<i>The Stone Sanctuary.</i>

07:03.464 --> 07:05.883
There are some metals
essential for building rocket engines

07:05.967 --> 07:08.052
that can't be found anywhere else too.

07:08.636 --> 07:11.013
There are lots of diamonds and other gems.

07:11.597 --> 07:14.934
<i>We'll build a city of super alloys there.</i>

07:15.560 --> 07:19.397
I see. The very place
where we aimed to build our town

07:19.480 --> 07:23.025
is also where we can get the diamond
materials for the Medusas' batteries.

07:23.109 --> 07:24.944
It's killing two birds with one stone.

07:27.447 --> 07:29.615
You knew it all along,
didn't you, dear Xeno?

07:29.699 --> 07:32.785
The final destination
of our South American expedition.

07:33.494 --> 07:37.206
There's no way
I'd miss the materials Senku needed.

07:37.290 --> 07:39.417
After all, we're both rocket scientists.

07:45.923 --> 07:49.719
Isn't there some weird noise
on the enemy's transmissions?

07:49.802 --> 07:51.721
It's a man-made noise.

07:52.472 --> 07:54.265
The peaks are intentionally consistent.

07:56.601 --> 07:59.520
<i>Xeno is sneaking a message for us</i>

08:00.146 --> 08:02.315
into the Science Club kids' signals.

08:02.899 --> 08:05.401
He's issuing some kind of alert signal.

08:06.777 --> 08:09.113
What kind of alert signal?

08:09.197 --> 08:13.242
Surely we can track down
the Science Club kids with this radar.

08:13.826 --> 08:16.621
The victory is in our hands.

08:16.704 --> 08:19.665
Jamming signals, stealth ships,

08:19.749 --> 08:21.584
anti-radar missiles...

08:22.293 --> 08:23.377
Are you serious?

08:24.879 --> 08:26.172
We can't rule those out,

08:26.255 --> 08:29.175
considering the nature of our enemies.

08:29.258 --> 08:32.762
Xeno isn't sounding the alarm
just because.

08:33.679 --> 08:37.266
<i>Back then, Xeno sent me a message
by blinking his eyes.</i>

08:38.017 --> 08:39.977
<i>Their destination is Araxá.</i>

08:41.103 --> 08:43.606
Turn around!
We won't chase them, we'll get ahead!

08:44.190 --> 08:46.651
We're off to the Senku crew's destination!

08:50.821 --> 08:53.866
Huh? Did we arrive already?

08:53.950 --> 08:54.784
Not yet.

08:55.576 --> 08:59.539
This is a super shortcut by land
to the Stone Sanctuary!

08:59.622 --> 09:00.873
ELEVATOR ACQUIRED!

09:00.957 --> 09:02.083
This is funsies!

09:02.166 --> 09:04.794
It's not funsies!
This is way too hard to take!

09:04.877 --> 09:07.046
It's a mountain, or rather, a cliff!

09:07.129 --> 09:09.590
Topographically, it's only the beginning.

09:09.674 --> 09:11.801
Once we get up, we'll take the bikes out!

09:11.884 --> 09:14.095
You say it's an elevator, but...

09:14.178 --> 09:16.305
isn't it just a ropeway set up vertically?

09:28.985 --> 09:32.405
<i>If I perfect the technique
for the diamond batteries,</i>

09:32.488 --> 09:36.409
<i>the petrification beam
will belong to humanity forever.</i>

09:39.996 --> 09:42.373
We'll get the diamonds
and make the batteries

09:42.456 --> 09:44.792
to make our trump card against Stanley

09:44.875 --> 09:49.547
in the form of deploying
a Medusa machine gun!

09:50.298 --> 09:53.301
The final battle will take place
deep in the Amazon,

09:54.010 --> 09:56.929
in the Stone Sanctuary!

10:06.147 --> 10:08.149
So we're about to see

10:08.232 --> 10:10.735
a mountain made of rare metals

10:10.818 --> 10:13.029
essential for building rockets!

10:13.696 --> 10:15.906
Why are there areas
so terribly convenient,

10:15.990 --> 10:18.659
they're like Earth's treasure chests?

10:20.077 --> 10:21.203
I'll show you.

10:26.959 --> 10:28.919
The gravel just separated by itself.

10:30.212 --> 10:33.007
It's called gravity separation.
Pretty amusing, don't you think?

10:33.591 --> 10:35.926
It's like heavy rocks just
pile up at the bottom.

10:36.010 --> 10:38.596
It's what they do
when they're looking for gold.

10:39.347 --> 10:41.515
<i>On a global scale, that process went on</i>

10:41.599 --> 10:44.101
<i>for tens of thousands of years,</i>

10:44.185 --> 10:47.438
<i>and what built up here
is the miraculous Araxá ore vein.</i>

10:48.939 --> 10:53.319
Earth... tens of thousands of years?
That's all too deep for me.

10:53.402 --> 10:55.696
Well, you see, the scale...

10:56.572 --> 10:58.074
<i>You know what they say.</i>

10:58.157 --> 11:02.495
<i>From the magnificent Earth's perspective,
humanity is but a parasite.</i>

11:03.788 --> 11:06.582
From my perspective
as someone who barely knows science,

11:07.333 --> 11:10.461
seeing all of humanity use
every bit of the Earth's power

11:10.544 --> 11:14.215
and build up millions of years
of wisdom to survive

11:14.757 --> 11:17.468
looks far more magnificent.

11:19.387 --> 11:22.181
You just said something beautiful, Kohaku!

11:22.264 --> 11:25.226
You're right! Let's make good use

11:25.309 --> 11:26.894
of the Earth's resources!

11:27.478 --> 11:31.565
We'll build together a city of alloys!

11:50.126 --> 11:54.964
So, what is this amazingly
solid fortress for?

11:55.047 --> 11:58.217
For the final battle against Stanley.

11:58.300 --> 11:59.427
- What?
- What?

11:59.510 --> 12:02.054
He's really chasing us all the way here?

12:02.138 --> 12:03.931
But can he actually find us here?

12:04.014 --> 12:07.059
I'm not scared at all, though...

12:08.727 --> 12:12.440
Xeno was mostly messing around
on the radio, you know.

12:13.315 --> 12:15.443
His nails are slightly magnetized.

12:16.318 --> 12:20.531
You were going
to call Stanley yourselves, right?

12:20.614 --> 12:22.491
Either way, it's the same thing.

12:22.575 --> 12:24.243
It's not the same.

12:24.326 --> 12:26.954
It is ridiculously early.

12:27.037 --> 12:28.205
I see.

12:28.289 --> 12:29.748
We want a ship!

12:29.832 --> 12:32.668
No, you don't get it.

12:32.751 --> 12:34.128
What are you even talking about?

12:34.211 --> 12:36.088
Back then,

12:36.172 --> 12:39.717
we needed a decent ship
to cross the vast ocean

12:39.800 --> 12:41.886
and sail around the globe.

12:42.470 --> 12:44.930
Of course, it's gotta be Perseus.

12:47.057 --> 12:51.312
We'll get Perseus back
to take Stanley down.

12:52.146 --> 12:56.567
<i>If Stanley beats us, he'll take Xeno back.</i>

12:57.234 --> 13:00.362
<i>The outcome will be decided here.</i>

13:01.363 --> 13:04.617
<i>Our goal in this race is the same.</i>

13:10.706 --> 13:15.252
What do you think about the fight
against Stanley, Tsukasa?

13:15.878 --> 13:17.129
Hyoga,

13:17.213 --> 13:21.175
The wound Stanley inflicted upon you
hasn't completely healed yet.

13:21.258 --> 13:22.510
The same goes for me...

13:23.260 --> 13:26.222
Our other fighters are Kohaku, Ukyo,

13:26.305 --> 13:27.890
and maybe Max.

13:30.100 --> 13:34.438
If they go head-to-head against
a fully-equipped US special forces unit,

13:34.522 --> 13:36.857
their chances of winning
are next to nothing.

13:37.858 --> 13:41.904
It looks like these are
our only chance of winning.

13:45.574 --> 13:47.117
<i>A Medusa machine gun.</i>

13:50.204 --> 13:52.456
It's not flying very far...

13:53.123 --> 13:56.877
Can we really beat Stanley with this?

13:56.961 --> 14:00.881
If they snipe us from long range,
it's game over.

14:01.590 --> 14:03.175
And then...

14:06.470 --> 14:08.973
<i>Fifty meters, one second!</i>

14:10.349 --> 14:14.270
What do you think of this trap?
I'll set them up everywhere.

14:17.481 --> 14:19.525
<i>Fifty meters, one second!</i>

14:20.150 --> 14:22.111
Accidental fire seems likely.

14:22.194 --> 14:24.321
We'll have to stock up big time.

14:24.989 --> 14:28.367
Strategies vary depending on the numbers.

14:28.450 --> 14:34.665
I wonder how many petrification devices
powered by diamonds we can prepare.

14:53.976 --> 14:56.020
Water jets are used to cut diamonds.

14:56.812 --> 14:59.106
I see, but let's not use firepower.

14:59.189 --> 15:00.858
We don't want to stand out.

15:00.941 --> 15:03.444
Yes, time for Kaseki's signature move!

15:03.527 --> 15:04.904
‎JACKED

15:05.529 --> 15:08.949
It doesn't really feel like that, though...

15:09.033 --> 15:12.369
This precious Medusa is a bit...

15:19.543 --> 15:22.046
The inside is too narrow!

15:22.129 --> 15:26.008
Is there really a way to open this
without breaking it into pieces?

15:26.091 --> 15:27.593
That's not all.

15:27.676 --> 15:30.888
We also have to take out a tiny diamond
and put another one in.

15:31.472 --> 15:35.893
Even the ace watchmaker Joel
is struggling to do it...

15:35.976 --> 15:40.689
Mass-producing machine guns with this
is certainly impossible.

15:40.773 --> 15:45.402
I wonder if we'll have one or two ready
by the time the enemy arrives.

15:45.986 --> 15:49.281
- Then...
- Fifty meters, three seconds!

15:49.865 --> 15:52.493
One, two, three...

15:52.576 --> 15:54.787
It's shining!

15:55.371 --> 15:58.040
There's a newly-crafted
battery diamond inside.

15:58.123 --> 15:59.208
I see!

15:59.291 --> 16:02.711
Even with just one, Ukyo's bow
can take them all out in one shot!

16:03.295 --> 16:06.757
Only if I have Stanley's position
on sight, though...

16:06.840 --> 16:09.218
Before I can get close enough to check,

16:09.301 --> 16:11.971
I'd probably be shot down.

16:13.013 --> 16:15.557
So I think I have an idea.

16:15.641 --> 16:18.185
In case we only had
one petrification device...

16:18.769 --> 16:21.522
I could carry it in my own hands

16:21.605 --> 16:25.359
and get close enough
to turn all of us into stone!

16:25.442 --> 16:29.196
One hundred meters, one second!

16:29.279 --> 16:32.908
Taiju, you mean you'll self-destruct?

16:32.992 --> 16:35.703
It will be fine! Just pour
the Revival Fluid on me afterwards!

16:36.328 --> 16:39.957
I'm ten billion percent sure they'll shoot
you before you get close enough, idiot.

16:40.040 --> 16:41.917
I'll push through!

16:42.001 --> 16:43.961
A headshot is a one-shot kill.

16:45.421 --> 16:46.714
<i>Self-destruct...</i>

16:49.258 --> 16:52.469
I think I have an idea.

16:52.553 --> 16:53.721
A sure-win one.

16:56.390 --> 16:58.392
We will all self-destruct!

16:59.893 --> 17:01.645
The moment Stanley gets here...

17:04.440 --> 17:07.609
Three thousand meters, one second!

17:09.028 --> 17:12.031
<i>As long as one of us is far enough...</i>

17:15.409 --> 17:18.370
<i>We'll just have to pour the Revival Fluid
on our allies only.</i>

17:20.831 --> 17:22.958
That's right! It will be messy, though.

17:23.042 --> 17:25.294
It will be a sure victory!

17:26.920 --> 17:28.922
There's only one problem.

17:29.548 --> 17:32.968
If the key person who has
the petrification device gets killed,

17:33.052 --> 17:35.846
not only will the operation fail,
but worse...

17:35.929 --> 17:38.348
Stanley will get the device.

17:38.432 --> 17:43.604
Don't worry about that.
We'll solve it with science.

17:45.939 --> 17:49.610
We'll put a Medusa and a speaker
in the watchtower.

17:49.693 --> 17:51.028
SPEAKER - SHOCK CANNON

17:51.111 --> 17:55.741
<i>We'll put mics everywhere
around the fortress.</i>

17:56.325 --> 17:58.327
<i>As soon as we're attacked,</i>

17:58.410 --> 18:01.121
<i>someone just needs to shout into a mic.</i>

18:01.205 --> 18:03.791
Three thousand meters, one second!

18:07.628 --> 18:10.839
This means we're turning
the abominable disaster

18:10.923 --> 18:14.885
that destroyed humanity
into its guardian deity.

18:14.968 --> 18:16.053
MEDUSA FORTRESS ACQUIRED!

18:16.136 --> 18:21.642
Yes. As soon as we
revive the petrification device,

18:21.725 --> 18:23.644
victory will be ours.

18:25.938 --> 18:28.690
How did you manage
to carefully dismantle the Medusa?

18:28.774 --> 18:30.859
It's all thanks to my great spirit.

18:31.443 --> 18:33.779
I failed thousands
of times before, though.

18:33.862 --> 18:36.782
So, just like Joel did,

18:36.865 --> 18:40.452
once you take out the diamond battery
and insert a new one, will it restart?

18:41.036 --> 18:42.955
I've tried, but it didn't work.

18:43.038 --> 18:46.416
Well, these artifacts
are thousands of years old.

18:47.000 --> 18:48.418
That's it...

18:48.502 --> 18:51.421
The only new one is the Medusa Joel has.

18:52.005 --> 18:57.052
In other words, the only thing I can do
is put my all into it.

18:57.136 --> 19:02.307
For the brand new,
perfectly shaped diamond batteries.

19:06.562 --> 19:09.481
One meter, one second.

19:10.607 --> 19:13.402
- It's not working, it's useless.
- Do not disturb!

19:13.986 --> 19:15.112
Hey!

19:16.196 --> 19:18.407
The shape and size are perfect.

19:18.991 --> 19:22.911
If my craftsmanship doesn't work,
no one else in the world can do this.

19:23.495 --> 19:25.873
It can't be done with average diamonds?

19:26.665 --> 19:28.542
Where did I go wrong?

19:30.127 --> 19:32.713
I haven't heard anything from Joel,

19:32.796 --> 19:37.092
and no matter how much I cut,
it feels like a complete waste of time.

19:37.176 --> 19:40.762
But I'm a gutsy guy,
so I'll keep giving it my all.

19:42.514 --> 19:45.767
- It wasn't a waste of time at all.
- Huh?

19:46.351 --> 19:48.520
Even Joel, the specialist,

19:48.604 --> 19:51.773
with a mountain of samples, can't do it.

19:52.399 --> 19:55.360
In other words,
we're doing something wrong.

19:55.444 --> 19:58.155
And that information is priceless.

19:59.948 --> 20:04.036
It's rare for you, Senku,
to be this good at encouraging people.

20:04.119 --> 20:05.454
Encouraging?

20:05.537 --> 20:09.583
Something like that wouldn't come out
of Senku's mouth in ten billion years.

20:09.666 --> 20:11.752
What kind of person is he thought to be?

20:11.835 --> 20:13.921
It's kinda true, though...

20:15.255 --> 20:18.008
Let me state a fact.

20:18.091 --> 20:20.552
This is the essence of trial and error.

20:20.636 --> 20:23.513
Accumulating errors isn't stagnation,
it's progress.

20:23.597 --> 20:24.556
I see!

20:25.140 --> 20:29.561
Even the smallest mistake with
the diamonds means game over, right?

20:31.396 --> 20:33.065
Wow, this is amazing!

20:33.148 --> 20:35.317
The diamond battery Kaseki made.

20:35.400 --> 20:36.985
It's incredibly tough!

20:37.736 --> 20:40.739
It's even better than the one
that came with the Medusa.

20:40.822 --> 20:44.076
Right? Aren't I awesome?

20:44.159 --> 20:46.745
I cut it in a way
that it will never crack!

20:46.828 --> 20:48.247
Huh?

20:49.164 --> 20:53.085
That's it.
I know this because I'm a craftsman.

20:53.168 --> 20:56.755
I can shape it so it won't crack
under normal conditions.

20:57.339 --> 20:58.840
Then, why?

20:59.800 --> 21:01.969
- There's a crack.
- That's true.

21:05.305 --> 21:11.186
If I strike the diamonds that came
from the Medusas just right like this...

21:13.647 --> 21:15.357
All the diamond batteries...

21:15.941 --> 21:18.777
Split perfectly in half.

21:19.486 --> 21:23.532
Could it be that the cleavage plane,
where molecular bonds are weak,

21:23.615 --> 21:25.617
runs along the line connecting the apexes?

21:27.160 --> 21:29.788
I wonder if anything
passes along that line.

21:29.871 --> 21:32.082
It could burn.

21:32.165 --> 21:35.168
This is a really tough process.

21:35.752 --> 21:38.046
<i>Hey, didn't we just discover</i>

21:38.130 --> 21:40.299
<i>the structure of the diamond batteries?</i>

21:41.758 --> 21:45.178
<i>I really want to show this to Joel.</i>

21:45.262 --> 21:48.265
<i>It'd be quicker if we told each other
what we found, right?</i>

21:48.849 --> 21:51.476
<i>We can't emit radio waves.</i>

21:51.560 --> 21:53.687
<i>Until we're ready to fight,</i>

21:53.770 --> 21:56.857
<i>we can't reveal our position
to Stanley's crew.</i>

21:56.940 --> 21:58.650
<i>It's a race against time.</i>

21:59.234 --> 22:02.279
The moment Stanley gets here,

22:02.362 --> 22:04.614
we'll be shot dead, right?

22:05.657 --> 22:08.493
Thus, if we get our Medusas
ready beforehand...

22:10.579 --> 22:12.456
Then victory will be ours.

23:53.348 --> 23:55.892
